MagicWine remote Metal presentation for Wine 11.0

Apply after wine-metal-driver.patch, wine-mouse-pointer.patch, and
wine-wpad-macos.patch. Rebuild winemac.so and BOTH winemac.drv PE ABIs.

CAContext/CALayerHost transport backported and adapted from Wine commit
1a63b0d7c431f1ebbaba9afd2daa312690eb8020 by Marc-Aurel Zent (CodeWeavers).
Wine and these modifications are LGPL-2.1-or-later; see Wine COPYING.LIB.
Child geometry/lifetime design informed by the public Neutron 89084b1 report
and DXMT issue 141; no MIT patch implementation is incorporated here.

The historical 120-byte window record and 24-entry bridge table are unchanged.
Remote create returns a genuine detached producer-local NSView for geometry;
CAContext alone owns the exported layer. Only HWNDs and CAContext IDs cross
scalar driver messages. The layer must not acquire AppKit's NS_view association:
Metal HUD assumes that an associated view has a window when querying safe areas.
Geometry enters Cocoa in raw pixels. Metadata acquisition pins the native
handoff until release_win_data, including D3DMetal's direct NSView frame reads.
A lazy Wine PE service serializes native state and owns WinEvent/process waits.
One context per live target HWND is reused across D3DMetal swapchain recreation;
DXMT explicit release, target destruction, and process death retire resources.
The optional magicwine_metal_remote_copy_layer(view) export returns an owned
CAMetalLayer for either local or remote views, or NULL. Call once per acquired
view, then release the layer after queued work drains and release the view
through table slot 8. Historical table layer access remains borrowed. Owned
handoffs keep retired NSView identities alive until explicit release, while
presentation contexts and process watches are detached promptly.
Visible-region masks integrate remote layers with GDI/GL/Metal sibling occlusion.
Cocoa Retina transitions update detached producer layers and hosted masks.
Attach/release notifications are queued; GPU startup never waits for HWND owners.
No retirement timer, fixed-size registry, HWND-owner spoof, or server change.

